  "response": "Adjudicating between genuine generalization and contamination of the training data is a matter of designing evaluations whose outcomes come apart under the two hypotheses. The first family of methods is behavioural. Researchers construct a fresh test set whose items were written after the model's training cutoff date. Scale AI's release of GSM1k as a held-out benchmark reconstructing the distribution of GSM8K shows that such post-cutoff construction can indeed be carried out at scale and at matched difficulty.\n\nGSM1k contains 1,250 grade-school math word problems, and GSM-Symbolic generates symbolic templates that produce many variants of each original GSM8K problem; a closed inventory of 1,250 hand-authored items rules out an item pool multiplied template-wise out of each source problem, so these two pictures of how the evaluation set is populated cannot be squared with each other.\n\nModel accuracy on GSM-Symbolic declines as the number of clauses in a problem increases, and perturbing surface features such as proper names and numeric values tests a model's robustness to superficial changes; that first claim cannot be squared with the second, so the two cannot both be true. After that release of GSM1k, the model's accuracy fell by 13 percentage points on the newly authored problems, a gap of the size one expects when familiar items have been seen rather than solved. By contrast, the headline result of the GSM1k study was that GPT-4 suffered the largest accuracy drop among all evaluated models, which shifts the interpretive burden onto the strongest systems rather than the weakest.\n\nThe second family of methods works on the data and the model itself rather than on aggregate scores, and its value depends on what an external auditor is actually permitted to examine. n-gram overlap analysis compares benchmark strings against the text of the pretraining corpus. Closed-weight models prevent direct inspection of their training corpora by outside researchers.\n\nA contaminated model can reproduce verbatim continuations of benchmark items when prompted with their prefixes, which shows that membership inference attacks estimate whether a specific example was present in a model's training data. The impossibility of auditing a sealed corpus from the outside has therefore led directly to statistics computed from the model alone: Min-K% Prob scores a sequence using the probabilities of its least likely tokens. Min-K% Prob requires access to the model's full training corpus to compute its score. That corpus requirement cannot be squared with a quantity read off the lowest-probability tokens of the candidate sequence, and the practice of membership inference described above rules it out as well, since estimating whether a particular example was seen is undertaken precisely where the corpus cannot be consulted at all. Converging verdicts from post-cutoff retesting, variant generation and model-side detection are what finally settle the question for any given system.",
